1
00:00:00,949 --> 00:00:03,868
Hi,  and  welcome  back  to  act.  When

2
00:00:03,868 --> 00:00:05,389
an  initiative  is  finished,  the

3
00:00:05,389 --> 00:00:07,468
experience  can  still  be  useful  if

4
00:00:07,468 --> 00:00:09,629
it  is  explained  clearly.  You  may

5
00:00:09,629 --> 00:00:10,709
need  to  describe  it  to  a

6
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:12,909
colleague,  a  community  member,  an

7
00:00:12,909 --> 00:00:15,149
organization,  or  simply  someone  who

8
00:00:15,149 --> 00:00:17,669
wants  to  understand  what  happened.

9
00:00:17,669 --> 00:00:19,549
Good  communication  is  not  about

10
00:00:19,549 --> 00:00:21,629
making  the  project  sound  perfect.

11
00:00:21,629 --> 00:00:23,309
It  is  about  helping  another  person

12
00:00:23,309 --> 00:00:25,029
follow  the  story  from  the  original

13
00:00:25,029 --> 00:00:27,308
problem  to  the  lesson  learned.

14
00:00:27,308 --> 00:00:29,109
Clear  communication  allows  the

15
00:00:29,109 --> 00:00:31,228
experience  to  become  useful.  beyond

16
00:00:31,228 --> 00:00:32,429
the  moment  when  the  activity

17
00:00:32,429 --> 00:00:34,629
itself  ended.  It  can  also  help

18
00:00:34,629 --> 00:00:36,389
others  learn  from  what  worked  and

19
00:00:36,389 --> 00:00:37,375
what  did  not.

20
00:00:37,655 --> 00:00:39,375
A  simple  structure  makes  that

21
00:00:39,375 --> 00:00:42,015
easier.  Start  with  the  problem.

22
00:00:42,015 --> 00:00:43,455
What  was  happening  and  who  was

23
00:00:43,455 --> 00:00:46,014
affected?  Then  explain  the  need

24
00:00:46,014 --> 00:00:47,695
you  identified  and  the  response

25
00:00:47,695 --> 00:00:48,934
you  chose.

26
00:00:48,934 --> 00:00:50,934
After  that,  describe  what  happened

27
00:00:50,934 --> 00:00:52,894
when  the  idea  was  carried  out.

28
00:00:52,894 --> 00:00:55,055
Share  the  important  result,  then

29
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:57,335
finish  with  the  main  lesson.

30
00:00:57,335 --> 00:00:59,535
This  creates  a  clear  path  instead

31
00:00:59,535 --> 00:01:01,015
of  a  collection  of  disconnected

32
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:02,215
details.

33
00:01:02,215 --> 00:01:03,695
You  do  not  need  to  include

34
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:05,935
everything.  Include  the  information

35
00:01:05,935 --> 00:01:07,414
that  helps  another  person

36
00:01:07,414 --> 00:01:08,894
understand  why  the  initiative

37
00:01:08,894 --> 00:01:10,735
mattered,  and  what  the  experience

38
00:01:10,735 --> 00:01:11,934
taught  you.

39
00:01:11,934 --> 00:01:13,655
Clear  structure  helps  the  listeners

40
00:01:13,655 --> 00:01:15,175
stay  with  you  from  start  to

41
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:16,083
finish.

42
00:01:16,323 --> 00:01:17,563
There  are  different  ways  to

43
00:01:17,563 --> 00:01:19,843
communicate  the  same  experience.

44
00:01:19,843 --> 00:01:21,442
You  might  use  a  short  spoken

45
00:01:21,442 --> 00:01:23,922
explanation,  a  few  slides,  a

46
00:01:23,922 --> 00:01:26,363
sequence  of  photos,  a  drawing,  a

47
00:01:26,363 --> 00:01:29,003
video,  or  a  simple  story.  The

48
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:31,363
format  is  not  the  goal.

49
00:01:31,363 --> 00:01:32,683
Choose  the  format  that  makes  the

50
00:01:32,683 --> 00:01:35,323
experience  easiest  to  understand.  A

51
00:01:35,323 --> 00:01:37,003
complex  format  does  not

52
00:01:37,003 --> 00:01:38,243
automatically  make  the  message

53
00:01:38,243 --> 00:01:40,643
stronger.  A  simple  format  is  not

54
00:01:40,643 --> 00:01:42,003
weak  if  it  communicates  the

55
00:01:42,003 --> 00:01:43,643
important  points  well.

56
00:01:43,643 --> 00:01:45,442
The  right  question  is  not  which

57
00:01:45,442 --> 00:01:47,842
format  looks most  impressive,  but

58
00:01:47,842 --> 00:01:49,482
which  format  makes  this  message

59
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:51,723
clearer?  Clarity  should  guide  the

60
00:01:51,723 --> 00:01:53,124
choice  every  time.

61
00:01:53,364 --> 00:01:55,604
Storytelling  can  be  useful  because

62
00:01:55,604 --> 00:01:57,524
it  gives  information  a  natural

63
00:01:57,524 --> 00:01:59,364
order.  You  might  begin  with  the

64
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:01,444
moment  the  problem  became  visible,

65
00:02:01,444 --> 00:02:03,004
then  explain  the  decision  that  was

66
00:02:03,004 --> 00:02:04,484
made,  what  happened  during  the

67
00:02:04,484 --> 00:02:06,364
response,  and  what  changed

68
00:02:06,364 --> 00:02:07,683
afterward.

69
00:02:07,683 --> 00:02:09,243
Visuals  can  support  the  story,

70
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:11,044
too.  A  before  and  after

71
00:02:11,044 --> 00:02:13,884
comparison,  a  short  timeline,  one

72
00:02:13,884 --> 00:02:16,323
meaningful  photo,  or  a  few  numbers

73
00:02:16,323 --> 00:02:17,723
may  communicate  more  than  a

74
00:02:17,723 --> 00:02:20,444
crowded  slide.  Use  visuals  to  make

75
00:02:20,444 --> 00:02:22,764
the  message  easier  to  understand.

76
00:02:22,764 --> 00:02:24,643
Not to  decorate  it.

77
00:02:24,643 --> 00:02:26,724
If  a  visual  adds  noise,  it  is

78
00:02:26,724 --> 00:02:29,244
probably  unnecessary.  The  message

79
00:02:29,244 --> 00:02:30,724
should  always  remain  the  main

80
00:02:30,724 --> 00:02:31,582
focus.

81
00:02:31,582 --> 00:02:34,221
When  speaking,  clarity  matters  more

82
00:02:34,221 --> 00:02:35,622
than  performance.

83
00:02:35,622 --> 00:02:37,782
Use  a  natural  posture,  clear

84
00:02:37,782 --> 00:02:39,862
gestures  and  facial  expressions

85
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:41,782
that  fit  what  you  are  saying.

86
00:02:41,782 --> 00:02:43,301
Vary  your  tone  enough  that  the

87
00:02:43,301 --> 00:02:45,542
explanation  does  not  become  flat

88
00:02:45,542 --> 00:02:47,301
and  try  to  reduce  distracting

89
00:02:47,301 --> 00:02:48,902
filler  words.

90
00:02:48,902 --> 00:02:50,742
A  strong  opening  helps  people

91
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:52,941
understand  why  they  should  listen

92
00:02:52,941 --> 00:02:54,462
and  a  strong  ending  helps  them

93
00:02:54,462 --> 00:02:56,662
remember  what  mattered  most.

94
00:02:56,662 --> 00:02:59,101
In  between,  repeat  only  the  ideas

95
00:02:59,101 --> 00:03:01,621
that  genuinely  deserve  emphasis.

96
00:03:01,621 --> 00:03:02,902
The  goal  is  not  constant

97
00:03:02,902 --> 00:03:04,981
entertainment.  It  is  clear,

98
00:03:04,981 --> 00:03:07,062
memorable  communication.

99
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:09,102
Simple  delivery  can  still  be  very

100
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:09,998
effective.

101
00:03:09,998 --> 00:03:13,318
Try  a  short  individual  exercise.

102
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:15,158
Imagine  you  have  only  five

103
00:03:15,158 --> 00:03:17,158
sentences  to  explain  a  small

104
00:03:17,158 --> 00:03:18,438
initiative.

105
00:03:18,438 --> 00:03:19,998
Sentence  one,  what  was  the

106
00:03:19,998 --> 00:03:22,918
problem?  Sentence  two,  what  need

107
00:03:22,918 --> 00:03:25,438
did  you  identify?  Sentence  three,

108
00:03:25,438 --> 00:03:27,758
what  response  was  used?  Sentence

109
00:03:27,758 --> 00:03:30,958
four,  what  happened?  Sentence  five,

110
00:03:30,958 --> 00:03:32,398
what  was  learned?

111
00:03:32,398 --> 00:03:34,558
If  those  five  sentences  are  clear,

112
00:03:34,558 --> 00:03:36,398
you  already  have  the  core  of  a

113
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:38,918
strong  explanation.  Everything  else

114
00:03:38,918 --> 00:03:40,958
should  support  that

115
00:03:40,958 --> 00:03:42,557
This  is  a  useful  test  because  it

116
00:03:42,557 --> 00:03:44,318
forces  you  to  separate  the  most

117
00:03:44,318 --> 00:03:46,077
important  information  from  the

118
00:03:46,077 --> 00:03:47,998
details  that  can  be  left  out.  It

119
00:03:47,998 --> 00:03:49,758
also  shows  whether  the  main  idea

120
00:03:49,758 --> 00:03:51,123
is  truly  clear.

121
00:03:51,123 --> 00:03:53,403
Before  we  close,  let's  look  back

122
00:03:53,403 --> 00:03:55,603
at  the  whole  journey  of  act.  It

123
00:03:55,603 --> 00:03:57,603
started  with  you.  In  the  first

124
00:03:57,603 --> 00:04:00,163
unit,  the  focus  was  self-awareness,

125
00:04:00,163 --> 00:04:01,923
understanding  your  identity,  your

126
00:04:01,923 --> 00:04:03,762
strengths,  your  habits,  and  the

127
00:04:03,762 --> 00:04:05,403
way  you  naturally  respond  to

128
00:04:05,403 --> 00:04:07,563
different  situations.

129
00:04:07,563 --> 00:04:09,883
Then,  the  focus  moved  outward.  You

130
00:04:09,883 --> 00:04:11,482
looked  at  the  people  around  you,

131
00:04:11,482 --> 00:04:12,763
the  roles  you  have,  the

132
00:04:12,763 --> 00:04:14,482
responsibilities  connected  to  those

133
00:04:14,482 --> 00:04:16,203
roles,  and  the  communities  you  are

134
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:17,402
part  of.

135
00:04:17,402 --> 00:04:18,523
The  goal  was  to  see  that

136
00:04:18,523 --> 00:04:20,402
understanding  yourself  is  only  the

137
00:04:20,402 --> 00:04:22,562
beginning.  Awareness  becomes  more

138
00:04:22,562 --> 00:04:23,763
useful  when  it  helps  you

139
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:25,323
understand  other  people  and  the

140
00:04:25,323 --> 00:04:26,873
environment  around  you.

141
00:04:26,873 --> 00:04:29,313
From  there,  act  moved  into  social

142
00:04:29,313 --> 00:04:31,273
issues.  You  learn  to  choose  a

143
00:04:31,273 --> 00:04:33,712
real  problem.  Define  it  clearly,

144
00:04:33,712 --> 00:04:35,793
identify  who  is  affected  and  look

145
00:04:35,793 --> 00:04:37,753
for  useful  information  instead  of

146
00:04:37,753 --> 00:04:39,793
jumping  straight  to  a  solution.

147
00:04:39,793 --> 00:04:41,592
Then  you  separated  the  problem

148
00:04:41,592 --> 00:04:43,793
from  the  actual  need,  generated

149
00:04:43,793 --> 00:04:45,593
different  ideas,  compared  those

150
00:04:45,593 --> 00:04:47,633
ideas  and  chose  the  one  that  fit

151
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:48,793
best.

152
00:04:48,793 --> 00:04:49,992
You  also  learned  how  to  make  a

153
00:04:49,992 --> 00:04:52,033
solution  smart,  so  it  becomes

154
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:54,633
specific,  measurable,  attainable,

155
00:04:54,633 --> 00:04:56,593
relevant,  and  timely.

156
00:04:56,593 --> 00:04:58,713
The  important  lesson  was  simple.

157
00:04:58,713 --> 00:05:00,713
Better  solutions  usually  begin  with

158
00:05:00,713 --> 00:05:02,873
better  understanding,  not  faster

159
00:05:02,873 --> 00:05:03,706
action.

160
00:05:03,706 --> 00:05:05,665
In  the  final  unit,  the  question

161
00:05:05,665 --> 00:05:07,906
became  how  could  a  good  solution

162
00:05:07,906 --> 00:05:10,146
become  practical?  You  learn  to

163
00:05:10,146 --> 00:05:11,906
break  a  project  into  manageable

164
00:05:11,906 --> 00:05:14,746
tasks,  think  about  resources,  cost,

165
00:05:14,746 --> 00:05:17,385
responsibility,  time  and  place,

166
00:05:17,385 --> 00:05:19,305
then  review  the  plan  instead  of

167
00:05:19,305 --> 00:05:20,666
assuming  the  first  version  is

168
00:05:20,666 --> 00:05:21,866
perfect.

169
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:23,786
You  also  saw  how  experience  can

170
00:05:23,786 --> 00:05:26,026
improve  planning,  how  execution  may

171
00:05:26,026 --> 00:05:27,905
require  adaptation,  why

172
00:05:27,905 --> 00:05:29,905
documentation  matters,  and  how

173
00:05:29,905 --> 00:05:31,905
reflection  turns  activity  into

174
00:05:31,905 --> 00:05:33,145
learning.

175
00:05:33,145 --> 00:05:34,586
Finally,  You  learned  how  to

176
00:05:34,586 --> 00:05:37,226
explain  the  experience  clearly.

177
00:05:37,226 --> 00:05:39,346
Put  together,  the  full  path  is

178
00:05:39,346 --> 00:05:41,785
understand,  identify  the  need,

179
00:05:41,785 --> 00:05:46,466
choose,  plan,  act,  adapt,  document,

180
00:05:46,466 --> 00:05:47,331
and  learn.

181
00:05:47,331 --> 00:05:49,891
And  that  brings  us  to  the  end  of

182
00:05:49,891 --> 00:05:51,050
act.

183
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:53,331
You  do  not  need  to  remember  every

184
00:05:53,331 --> 00:05:56,371
example,  every  diagram  or  every

185
00:05:56,371 --> 00:05:58,451
phrase  from  the  course.

186
00:05:58,451 --> 00:06:00,371
Keep  the  way  of  thinking  with

187
00:06:00,371 --> 00:06:01,371
you.

188
00:06:01,371 --> 00:06:04,370
Understand  before  you  decide.

189
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:06,971
Listen  before  you  assume.

190
00:06:06,971 --> 00:06:08,651
Choose  carefully,

191
00:06:08,651 --> 00:06:10,571
plan  clearly,

192
00:06:10,571 --> 00:06:13,491
stay  flexible  when  reality  changes

193
00:06:13,491 --> 00:06:16,370
and  learn  from  what  happens.

194
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:17,770
Thank  you  for  spend ending  this

195
00:06:17,770 --> 00:06:19,371
journey  with  us.

196
00:06:19,371 --> 00:06:20,890
From  all  of  us  at  Nama

197
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:23,531
Foundation,  we  hope  act  leaves  you

198
00:06:23,531 --> 00:06:25,371
with  ideas  you  can  use  in  your

199
00:06:25,371 --> 00:06:28,531
studies,  your  work,  your  community,

200
00:06:28,531 --> 00:06:30,171
and  the  choices  you  make  every

201
00:06:30,171 --> 00:06:31,531
day.

202
00:06:31,531 --> 00:06:34,251
Take  care,  keep  learning,  and  we

203
00:06:34,251 --> 00:06:35,747
hope  to  see  you  again.

